window.docContents[226].resolve({"/The-Proved-Spine/Examination-___-principle___-method___-procedure-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-7___-14_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Examination-___-principle___-method___-procedure-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-7___-14_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nThe examination chain has three layers — an examination principle (§7.5 / VIM4\n2CD 2.4), a method based on a principle (VIM4 2CD 2.5), and a procedure based\non a method (VIM4 2CD 2.7) — gathered into one carrier ExaminationItem so that refinement\nis a homogeneous relation. The forgetful projection basePrinciple sends any\nitem down to the principle it rests on.\n\nThree small structures with DecidableEq, united by an ExaminationItem\ninductive; basePrinciple is the projection to the principle layer.\n\nAn examination method (Dybkær Ch. 7 / VIM4 2CD 2.5) is a generic description of\nan examination, based on an examination principle.\n\nA structure carrying an identifier and the ExaminationPrinciple it is based on.\n\nAn examination procedure (Dybkær Ch. 7 / VIM4 2CD 2.7) is a detailed description,\nbased on an examination method; its principle is\nthe method's principle.\n\nA structure carrying an identifier and the ExaminationMethod it is based on.\n\nRefinement Refines is the reflexive–transitive closure of the \"based-on\"\nedges (a procedure is based on its method, a method on its principle): procedure\n⊑ method ⊑ principle. It mirrors specialization over\nkinds — the same closure construction, one level down at the defining aspect.\n\nAn inductive with refl and step constructors over a BasedOn edge\nrelation; of_basedOn lifts a single edge.\n\nRefinement is a preorder. It is reflexive (the refl constructor) and\ntransitive (Refines.trans) on the examination chain — the\nsame algebraic law proved for specialization, which an OWL2 reasoner can neither\nstate nor prove.\n\nTransitivity is by induction on the first derivation, exactly as for\nSpecializes: refl returns the second derivation, step re-applies the\nconstructor under the inductive hypothesis.\n\nRefinement preserves the principle. Every examination in one refinement chain\nrests on the same base principle:\n\nThe forgetful projection to the principle layer is invariant along refinement —\nthe examination-layer analogue of  being a homomorphism, and one provable\nhere in the Mathlib-free core without PhysLib. Builds on refinement.\n\nBy induction on the refinement derivation: each BasedOn edge preserves the base\nprinciple (cases … <;> rfl), and the inductive step chains the resulting\nequalities.\n\nThe examination principle is a defining aspect. Two kinds\nexamined by different principles are distinct kinds — independently of scale and\ndimension. This is what keeps width and height distinct while both remain\nrational lengths, mutually comparable under their\nshared super-kind Length.\n\nThe kinds differ in their examPrinciple field, so they are unequal by\ncongruence: substitute the assumed kind-equality and derive a contradiction from\nthe field inequality.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009The Proved Spine","header":"5.4. Examination — principle, method, procedure (Dybkær Ch. 7, 14)","id":"/The-Proved-Spine/Examination-___-principle___-method___-procedure-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-7___-14_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--The-Proved-Spine--Examination-___-principle___-method___-procedure-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-7___-14_RPAR_"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-four-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-four-steps--Step-4-___-Differentiate-shared-kinds___-dedicate___-or-index-by-field":{"contents":"Finally, decide what to do about quantities that share a kind. There are two cases, and they go on\ndifferent axes.When two quantities should be different kinds because their measurement target differs — same\nquantity, different system or component — make them distinct with a \ndedicated kind: holding the component (or system) apart\nis enough to force distinct kinds, without inventing\nidentity strings. In the example the bound-water and free-water static permittivities\n(boundStaticPerm, freeStaticPerm) are one relative-permittivity kind dedicated to two\ncomponents, hence provably distinct.When the algebra forces a genuinely shared kind, the leftover distinction is positional, not\nmetrological, and belongs on a second axis carried by named record fields — not arrays. One row\nof the Cholesky factor — the example's CholRowQ α — is row-homogeneous: three fields\nl20 l21 l22, all Quantity reflectivity α, because  makes every\n land at the same kind regardless of the column — a kind that separated them would\nmake the factorization's own row sums ill-typed. The remaining \"which slot\" distinction is held by\nthe distinct field names and checked off the kind axis by an erasure rfl: the theorem\ncholRowQ_magnitudes proves the kinded row's .magnitudes are definitionally the bare-Float\nkernel's outputs, so a same-kind slot swap still type-checks but breaks parity. A Fin 3-indexed\narray would push that positional bookkeeping onto the type and discard the named slots; the record\nkeeps both axes separable — kind by the elaborator, position by parity-to-reference.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009The four steps","header":"15.1.4. Step 4 — Differentiate shared kinds: dedicate, or index by field","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-four-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-four-steps--Step-4-___-Differentiate-shared-kinds___-dedicate___-or-index-by-field"}});